      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{a}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{a}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{a}+2}{\\sqrt{a}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{a}+2}{(\\sqrt{a}-2)(\\sqrt{a}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{a}+2}{(\\sqrt{a})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{a}+2}{a-4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
